  "description": "The safe AVX2 M4RM Gray-table wrappers accept arguments for which their kernels write outside the caller's buffer; a safe function never does.\n\n## Background\n\n`m4rm_gray_build4_fn` and `m4rm_gray_build8_fn` in `crates/gf2-kernels-simd/src/x86/avx2.rs` are safe functions published through the `LogicalFns` function-pointer table. Each calls an `unsafe` kernel that stores table entry 0 unconditionally and then stores at the Gray-code index `i ^ (i >> 1)` for every `i` below `table_size`. That index stays below `table_size` only when `table_size` is a power of two. The wrappers assert the stride, `buffer.len() >= table_size * stride_words` and `panel.len() >= valid_rows * stride_words`, with unchecked multiplications, and do not assert a power-of-two table size. A caller passing `table_size = 0` with an empty buffer, or `table_size = 3` with a buffer of three entries, reaches an out-of-bounds store from safe code. The one production caller, `build_gray_table_flat` in `crates/gf2-core/src/alg/m4rm.rs`, passes `1 << k_block`, so shipped behaviour stays in bounds. The `M4rmGrayBuildFn` type documents panics and states no such precondition.\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n- [hard] REQ-01: Each wrapper panics, before any store, for a `table_size` that is zero or not a power of two and for argument combinations whose required lengths overflow `usize`; tests written before the change show the present behaviour and pass after it.\n- [hard] REQ-02: The scalar reference and every other implementation behind `M4rmGrayBuildFn` enforce the same preconditions, shown by one shared test over all implementations, and the type's rustdoc states them under Panics.\n- [hard] REQ-03: For every valid argument set the table contents are unchanged, shown by the shared M4RM suites with and without the `simd` feature on Rust 1.95.\n- [hard] REQ-04: The regenerated assembly listing of each changed SIMD source is committed with the source change.\n\n## Notes\n\nFound while stating the kernel crate's safety contracts. Validation here catches caller mistakes at a safe boundary; it adds no unchecked fast path.\n",
